<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Before you start</h2>
	<ol>
		<li><strong>A VoiceML account with a phone number.</strong> See <a href="/voiceml/">VoiceML</a> or <a href="/signup/">start a free trial</a>.</li>
		<li><strong>Four extensions registered on your SIP domain</strong>, numbered 100 to 103.</li>
		<li><strong>The VoiceML Dashboard connected to the account.</strong> An administrator signs in at the <a href="{{ site.voicemlDashboardUrl }}">VoiceML Dashboard</a>, verifies the account with its Account SID and API key, and invites each address that receives messages from the Users page. The dashboard refuses callbacks for an address that isn&rsquo;t a member of the account.</li>
		<li><strong>For the AI agent, your agent on a <code>wss://</code> WebSocket.</strong> The relay needs a speech recognizer and a synthesis engine on the account, or the platform defaults. See <a href="/voiceml/conversationrelay/">ConversationRelay</a>.</li>
	</ol>
	<div class="alert alert-warning" role="note">
		<p><strong>widgets.com is used here as a fictional company.</strong> It is a real, registered domain with no connection to VoiceTel. Replace every email address and the SIP domain with your own before you publish.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Step 1: Publish the goodbye document</h2>
	<p>Every <code>&lt;Record&gt;</code> needs an <code>action</code>. Without one, the runtime requests the current document again when the recording ends, which replays the voicemail greeting and records again. This small document is that <code>action</code>: it tells a knock-knock joke and hangs up.</p>
	<p class="code-block-header">goodbye.xml</p>
	<pre class="code-block" data-lang="xml"><code>{{ voicemlSamples["widgets-pbx"].goodbye | escape }}</code></pre>
	<p>Paste it at <a href="{{ site.voicemlPublisherUrl }}">voiceml.org</a>, validate it, and publish. It contains no Liquid, so it is served from an <code>/s/</code> URL. Keep the URL exactly as returned, <code>key</code> included. The key is a shared secret, so don&rsquo;t post the URL publicly.</p>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Step 2: Publish the main document</h2>
	<p>In the document below, replace <code>GOODBYE_URL</code> (five places) with the URL from step 1, and <code>wss://YOUR_AI_WEBSOCKET_URL</code> with your agent&rsquo;s endpoint. Replace the email addresses, names, and SIP addresses with your own.</p>
	<p class="code-block-header">main.xml</p>
	<pre class="code-block" data-lang="xml"><code>{{ voicemlSamples["widgets-pbx"].main | escape }}</code></pre>
	<p>Publish it at voiceml.org. Because it contains Liquid tags, it is published to an <code>/r/</code> URL and rendered on every call with that call&rsquo;s parameters.</p>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Step 3: Point the number at it</h2>
	<p>Set the <code>/r/</code> URL as the phone number&rsquo;s voice URL. GET and POST both work.</p>
	<p>To log calls that never reach a <code>&lt;Dial&gt;</code>, such as calls to the AI agent or callers who hang up during the menu, also point the number&rsquo;s status callback at the dashboard&rsquo;s <code>/status/</code> route for a recipient. A number&rsquo;s status callback reports only the completed event, so the dashboard records those calls when they hang up.</p>
	<p>Then test it: call the number, press 1, and watch the call appear on the dashboard&rsquo;s live board while extension 100 rings. Answer it, drag the call onto another extension, and start a live transcript from the call view. Call again and let it ring out to check that the voicemail and its transcript arrive by email.</p>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>How the routing works</h2>
	<p>The first request for the main document carries no <code>Digits</code>, so <code>{% raw %}{% if Digits == blank %}{% endraw %}</code> plays the greeting inside a <code>&lt;Gather&gt;</code>. That <code>&lt;Gather&gt;</code> has no <code>action</code>, so when the caller presses keys the runtime requests the same document again with <code>Digits</code> set, and the <code>{% raw %}{% else %}{% endraw %}</code> branch routes the call. <code>timeout="3"</code> ends input three seconds after the last key, which gives callers time to dial a three-digit extension; pressing <kbd>#</kbd> ends it sooner. A caller who presses nothing hears &ldquo;We did not receive a selection&rdquo; and the call ends.</p>
	<div class="table-container">
		<table class="data-table">
			<caption>What each entry does</caption>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th scope="col">Caller enters</th>
					<th scope="col">Rings</th>
					<th scope="col">Recordings and voicemail go to</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>1 or 100</td><td>Thornton Melon, extension 100</td><td>thornton@widgets.com</td></tr>
				<tr><td>2</td><td>Extensions 100, 101, 102, and 103 together</td><td>thornton@widgets.com</td></tr>
				<tr><td>101</td><td>Lou, extension 101</td><td>lou@widgets.com</td></tr>
				<tr><td>3 or 102</td><td>Jason Melon, extension 102</td><td>jason@widgets.com</td></tr>
				<tr><td>4 or 103</td><td>Dr. Diane Turner, extension 103</td><td>diane@widgets.com</td></tr>
				<tr><td>0</td><td>The AI agent</td><td>Not recorded by this document</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Anything else</td><td>Nothing; the caller hears &ldquo;That is not a valid option&rdquo;</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p>Every branch that rings a phone follows the same pattern:</p>
	<ol>
		<li>It announces that the call is being recorded.</li>
		<li><code>&lt;Dial record="record-from-answer-dual"&gt;</code> rings the extension for 20 seconds and sends the two-channel recording to the dashboard&rsquo;s <code>/recording/</code> route.</li>
		<li>The <code>statusCallback</code> on <code>&lt;Sip&gt;</code> sends ringing, answered, and completed events to <code>/status/</code>, which puts the call on the live board.</li>
		<li>If nobody answers, <code>&lt;Record transcribe="true"&gt;</code> takes a voicemail and sends the audio to <code>/voicemail/</code> and the transcript to <code>/transcript/</code>. Both use the same address, so the dashboard joins them into one message.</li>
	</ol>
	<p>Each callback URL also carries <code>From</code>, <code>To</code>, and <code>Digits</code>, which the dashboard shows with the message as call context. Every value placed inside a URL ends in <code>| url_encode</code>, so a caller&rsquo;s <code>+</code> or <code>&amp;</code> can&rsquo;t break the query string.</p>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Running the switchboard</h2>
	<p>Operators and administrators land on the dashboard&rsquo;s operator panel. The summary strip shows calls active now, today&rsquo;s calls, answered calls with the answer rate, missed calls, and the average answered call. The live board lists calls in progress, and the Extensions card shows each extension as registered, ringing, on a call, or offline. Clicking a call opens its call view, with details, its live transcript, and these actions:</p>
	<div class="table-container">
		<table class="data-table">
			<caption>Call control from the VoiceML Dashboard</caption>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th scope="col">Action</th>
					<th scope="col">What happens</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>Hang up</td><td>Ends the call.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Transfer</td><td>Blind-transfers the caller to a phone number or SIP address. Dragging the call onto an extension does the same.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Send to voicemail</td><td>Plays a prompt and records a voicemail for the call&rsquo;s recipient.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Start or stop recording</td><td>Records both sides on separate channels and delivers the recording to Messages.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Start or stop live transcript</td><td>Transcribes both sides; the text streams into the call view as the call goes on.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p>Every action, including refused ones, is written to the audit log. Voicemail, recordings, and transcripts collect in Messages, with filters by number, a transcript search, and a filter for messages that carry what the caller pressed.</p>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Adding an extension</h2>
	<ol>
		<li>Register the new phone on your SIP domain, for example as extension 104.</li>
		<li>Copy one of the single-extension branches and change its <code>elsif</code> condition, <code>email</code>, <code>name</code>, and SIP address.</li>
		<li>Invite the new email address in the dashboard.</li>
		<li>Publish the updated document and point the number at its new <code>/r/</code> URL. Published documents can&rsquo;t be edited, so every change is a new URL.</li>
	</ol>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Limits of this design</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>The voicemail prompt can follow an answered call.</strong> The <code>&lt;Dial&gt;</code> has no <code>action</code>, so the document continues to the voicemail prompt whatever the outcome. If the extension hangs up first, the caller hears the prompt. Choosing what happens by dial result needs a server.</li>
		<li><strong>Ring-all calls don&rsquo;t show while they ring.</strong> The four <code>&lt;Sip&gt;</code> nouns behind option 2 have no <code>statusCallback</code>. Add the same <code>statusCallback</code> attributes used in the other branches to show those legs on the live board.</li>
		<li><strong><code>CallerName</code> arrives empty.</strong> The VoiceML runtime doesn&rsquo;t send it. <code>From</code> and <code>To</code> identify the caller.</li>
		<li><strong>Long voicemails may not get a transcript.</strong> Recordings are transcribed when they last between 2 and 120 seconds. With <code>maxLength="120"</code>, a message that runs the full length is at the limit; lower it to 115 if every voicemail needs a transcript.</li>
		<li><strong>No lookups or memory.</strong> A voiceml.org document can branch on the call itself but can&rsquo;t look anything up or remember anything between calls. A CRM lookup or caller authentication needs a server.</li>
	</ul>
</section>

